Here's a self-contained Python function that sets the row index of a table `df0` to a two-level hierarchical index using the values in columns `"str0"` and `"str1"`. It uses the multiindexer library if a helper is available; otherwise it falls back to pandas' set_index. The function returns a new object (pandas DataFrame or whatever the multiindexer helper returns).

Note: because the multiindexer public API may vary across versions, this function first tries a multiindexer helper (`mi.set_index`) if present, then falls back to pandas. If you know your multiindexer API (for example a specific method to set the index), you can replace the attempted calls with the exact API call.

